      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-159785",
      ":question/problem": "Suppose $X_{1}, X_{2}, \u2026, X_{12}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_{i}\u223cN(\u03b8, 1)$, where $\u03b8$ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample $x_{1}, x_{2}, \u2026, x_{12}$, you're given that\n$\u2211_(i = 1)^(12)x_{i} = 7.4, \u2211_(i = 1)^(12)x_{i}^{2} = 16.8$.\nGiven that the likelihood function of the sample is\n$L(\u03b8) = \\frac{1}{64\u03c0^{6}}e^{g (\u03b8)}$,\nwhat is the expression for $g(\u03b8)$?\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\u03b8$ and variance $1$ is given by*\n$f(x; \u03b8) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\u03c0}}e^{- (x - \u03b8)^{2}/2}, x \u2208 (- \u221e, \u221e)$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
    },
